  "transcript": "# Ofcom on the Online Safety Act: Progress, Enforcement and Reform\n\n## Implementation Status\n> Most Online Safety Act duties are in force, with categorised services expected by mid-2027, but new social media bans may require extra funding and sequencing.\n\nChair: Could you outline which parts of the Online Safety Act are now active and what's still pending?\nOliver Griffiths: We're balancing implementation, compliance, and new duties like the social media ban. Mark can detail the timeline.\nMark Bunting: Illegal harms and children's protection duties have been in force since early 2025. Categorised services were delayed by a judicial review but should be fully operational by mid-2027.\nMark Bunting: We're also implementing new offences from the Crime and Policing Act and consulting on a 48-hour takedown duty for intimate images.\nChair: What about the impact of the Government's social media ban for under-16s on your timetable?\nOliver Griffiths: We were already fully loaded, so we've moved staff to the ban work. The Government has promised extra funding, and we're in detailed discussions to sequence everything. It's doable with the right resources.\n\n\n## Lessons Learned and Engagement\n> Ofcom learned to engage more collaboratively with industry and civil society, but the safe harbour provision limits enforcement flexibility.\n\nChair: What lessons have you learned from implementing the first phases, and how are they shaping the next category 1 duties?\nMark Bunting: We've learned to engage more with stakeholders. For example, our work on women and girls was collaborative, and we're doing the same for transparency reports. But the safe harbour means if companies follow our codes, they're deemed compliant even if risks remain.\nCommittee Member: The Children's Commissioner said you wouldn't share risk assessments with her. Why?\nOliver Griffiths: We haven't received a formal request. Under Section 393, we need company consent to share. If she formally asks, we'll liaise with companies to see if they agree.\nChair: So if she makes a formal request, you'll facilitate?\nOliver Griffiths: Yes, we'll follow the process and check with the companies.\n\n\n## Impact on Safety\n> Age checks have increased and some services have improved, but overall impact on children's safety is still underwhelming, with AI supercharging harms.\n\nCommittee Member: Has the Act made people safer online?\nOliver Griffiths: We've seen big changes: 76% of companies completed risk assessments, and age checks have surged, especially on porn sites. But we're underwhelmed by the overall effect so far, especially on big services used by children.\nCommittee Member: The Children's Commissioner said the Act has made 'absolutely no difference'. How do you respond?\nOliver Griffiths: We share the frustration. This is about changing corporate culture, which takes time. We're at an early point, but we're driving change.\nMark Bunting: We've seen steps, like commitments from X, Meta, and Snap, but there's a long way to go. For example, they need better ways to identify child users to make systems work.\nCommittee Member: Should tech companies be allowed to operate if they can't guarantee children's safety?\nOliver Griffiths: Society says no. The Act puts duties on companies, and we can enforce, including business disruption measures that block UK access if they don't comply.\n\n\n## Gaming and Proactive Tech\n> Gaming has seen some age gates and controls, but Ofcom's focus is narrow; proactive monitoring tools are key but require overcoming legal thresholds.\n\nCommittee Member: What progress in gaming?\nOliver Griffiths: Services like Xbox and Steam have introduced age gates, but it's not our top priority. Social media and children's protection come first.\nMark Bunting: We've focused on user interactions, like on Roblox, where age verification and controls have been effective. But broader issues like violence and screen time aren't covered by the Act.\nCommittee Member: The Molly Rose Foundation says exposure to harmful content hasn't changed. What's stopping you?\nMark Bunting: We need proactive monitoring tools, but the Act requires us to prove they're accurate and effective before mandating them. We're consulting on this and will say more in autumn.\nCommittee Member: Isn't that a fundamental flaw?\nMark Bunting: It's a tension between privacy and safety. We think the case for tools is strong, and we're working on proposals.\n\n\n## Enforcement Challenges\n> Ofcom has opened many investigations and imposed fines, but legal challenges, overseas services, and non-payment hinder enforcement.\n\nCommittee Member: Stakeholders say Ofcom is slow and reactive. Why?\nOliver Griffiths: We move quickly when needed, like with Grok. But the Act covers 100,000 services, so we must prioritise. We face judicial reviews and appeals, which slow us down.\nCommittee Member: Do you have enough powers?\nSuzanne Cater: We're using our teeth. We've opened six enforcement programmes and 40 investigations, imposing £7 million in fines. But many services are overseas with no UK assets, making penalties hard to collect.\nSuzanne Cater: Business disruption measures are tough because we can only apply them for ongoing non-compliance. If a service withdraws from the UK, we lose jurisdiction.\nCommittee Member: How much of the £7 million has been paid?\nSuzanne Cater: I don't have the figure, but the majority hasn't been paid. We'll follow up.\nOliver Griffiths: As we fine bigger companies with UK assets, this will become less of an issue.\n\n\n## VPNs and International Comparisons\n> VPN use to evade age checks is a concern but not widespread; Ofcom can't require providers to block VPNs without legislative change.\n\nCommittee Member: Should the Act be amended to force companies to deal with VPNs?\nOliver Griffiths: VPN use increased but not massively. This is a political decision for Government. We'll do what we're told.\nCommittee Member: The Minister said providers must account for VPNs in age assurance. Do you agree?\nMark Bunting: They don't need to prevent VPN use. We've acted against firms encouraging VPNs, but requiring blocks would be a significant intervention needing debate.\n\n\n## Reform Suggestions\n> Ofcom suggests targeted reforms: a duty to mitigate all risks, better business disruption measures, and easier use of proactive tech.\n\nCommittee Member: Would you support removing the safe harbour?\nMark Bunting: It's complicated. Safe harbour gives legal certainty but limits our ability to enforce broader risk mitigation. A duty to mitigate all risks would help, but enforcement would be challenging.\nCommittee Member: What about a duty of care?\nSuzanne Cater: It's an alternative, but we'd face similar challenges in defining parameters. Safe harbour lets big services argue they're compliant even if harms persist.\nCommittee Member: If you could change one thing, what would it be?\nMark Bunting: Empower us to pressure companies to address all identified risks, linked to a duty of care.\nSuzanne Cater: Fix business disruption measures to make them more practical.\nOliver Griffiths: Make it easier to mandate proactive technology to control content served to users.\n"
